Claims identified & fact-check:

Claim 1: Children raised with iPads, flashing images, and addictive video games develop attention problems that get mistaken for ADHD, when the real issue is a lack of discipline
→ Unsupported/mischaracterized — this causal narrative (screen exposure → discipline failure → falsely labeled ADHD) is not addressed in DSM-5-TR or ICD-11. ICD-11's essential features require symptoms to be persistent, pervasive across multiple settings, and present from before age 12 — a pattern of environmentally induced boredom-driven inattention in one context (a "boring textbook") does not meet this threshold, and no reference material supports screen use or lack of discipline as an underlying cause or mimicker of ADHD

Claim 2: A child not wanting to pay attention to something boring (versus a stimulating video game) reflects a discipline problem rather than a genuine attention disorder, and diagnosing it as ADHD is "refusing to discipline your child"
→ Unsupported/mischaracterized — this directly contradicts ICD-11 and NICE. ICD-11 does note inattention may be less evident during highly stimulating activities, but this is described as one feature of the disorder, not evidence the disorder is fake or reducible to discipline. NICE 1.3.1 requires diagnosis to be made only by a trained specialist based on full clinical assessment, developmental history, and cross-setting observation — explicitly not something addressable by parental discipline alone, and NICE's "boundary with normality" criteria (via ICD-11) already account for and exclude behavior that is simply typical variation, undermining the claim that legitimate diagnoses are just mislabeled discipline failures

